{"kind":"report","text":"Approved: exact head aca376f750b29f13c858e254c135456caa27c5d9 on builder/ar1-archive-wake-20261116, all 10 artifacts, for request c0670f41 under my promise 79cbf34f. This verdict names the one implementation: AR-1 (request ba2c2fdb, promise 13bdd599, report 9979755c, the AR-1 note, primary). Main e34c51df is the base. `git merge-tree --write-tree e3392a05 aca376f7` merges cleanly onto the current main, with tree 4951598b. This head answers my changes-requested verdict 833510ac, under the planner's decisions 6fad3324 and d0be1aeb.\n\n## The wake rate, as my rule asks\n\n- The floor between wakes. Every archive retry waits at least 60 s and doubles with its stored count, up to 3600 s (`ARCHIVE_RETRY_FLOOR_SECONDS`, `ARCHIVE_RETRY_MAX_SECONDS`). The count lives in the wake record, so eviction does not reset it. Progress (a target, a Mark, a confirmation or `clearWake()`) drops it.\n- What stops a reschedule loop:\n  - `#retryWake` never leaves the stored wake at or before now.\n  - It returns the stored wake when that wake is in the future, so the in-memory fallback can no longer undercut it. That was my finding 1.\n  - A pass whose stored wake is in the future stops at step `wake-check`, right after the terminal check. It reads only the wake index from local SQL and returns that wake. It calls no policy, target or provider port. That was my finding 2, for a backed-off room.\n- Requests per room per hour. For a room whose archive cannot start: at most 6 passes in the first hour and then 1 an hour, at about 7 Durable Object requests a pass. That is about 42 in the first hour and about 7 an hour after. Alarms from other owners add no archive requests while the wake is in the future.\n- Requests for an idle room whose archive confirmed and cleared its wake. Each alarm from another owner still runs a full pass, with up to about six Directory calls. For M such alarms an hour on a Worker, that is up to about 6 × M requests an hour. M is not measured. The planner chose this in d0be1aeb, so that policy revocation and stored-row migration do not wait for an act. The builder's attempt to return `idle` failed 17 archive tests of exactly those kinds. The note now states this cost honestly, with the planner's ceiling and rollback: 150 Durable Object requests a minute on dap-grow and 50 on dap-lending, sustained over 10 minutes in the first hour, rolls both Workers back to the hotfix versions cef83935 and 70571e5b. I accept the decision on those terms. That ceiling is the only bound on M, so the first hour's read of the rate is a required step of this deployment, not an option.\n\n## What I checked\n\n- The source diff from d9bf1a5f to this head:\n  - The `#retryWake` future branch returns `stored`.\n  - The new `wake-check` step reads `rows.storedWake(budget)` and returns early when that wake is in the future and not stopped.\n  - A missing, cleared, due or stopped wake continues to the full pass.\n  - A pending Mark with a future `row.next` is caught by the same index, because `#save` writes `next` into the wake index.\n  - `signal()` still makes a wake due after an act, unless a deferral is pending.\n- The hour test now has 12 cases: two kinds, with and without the old wake, in three modes. In `other-alarms` mode, another owner's alarm fires every 30 s. On each such pass the test asserts that no port is called and the alarm time is unchanged, and it requires more than 50 such passes. `resident` keeps the object in memory, so the fallback takes part. The gaps stay 60, 120, 240, 480 and 960 s in every mode.\n- `archive-driver.test.ts` now asserts that a second run before the retry time returns that wake with no owner call.\n- The paired control. `/Users/hughpyle/play/artroom-evidence/2026-11-16-ar1b-gate/control-first-head-d9bf1a5f.log` fails the 2 pending-policy `other-alarms` cases with \"a pass that is not due calls no policy or target port: expected 2 to be 1\". The other 10 cases pass there. The red matches my finding.\n- The gate, END 967028da, ran on c4af141f with tree f3f1aa5d. Plain 4994 with 1 skipped and Native 1187 with 2 skipped passed, the 12 hour cases and the archive scale cases included. Lost crossings were 0, and the log ends \"gate passed\". aca376f7 changes only the note (`git diff --stat c4af141f aca376f7`: 1 file), so the notes-only rule covers it.\n- Scope. The 10 artifacts name the head, and their paths equal `git diff --no-renames --name-only e34c51df aca376f7`.\n- Cheap checks. The commit-message check passes: 6 checked, 0 flagged. `check-notes` gives 0 warnings, and `git diff --check` is clean. I ran no tests.\n\nArchitecture: sound. The backoff lives in stored state. The cheap not-due check comes before any port, and the full pass keeps its role for work that no act signals.\n\nSecurity: no finding. `apps/room/worker.ts` returns to `if (!config) return deployed;`, the bytes before the hotfix. The seals match the reviewed bytes. A revoked policy or removed trust still stops an idle room on its next full pass. On a room in backoff, that waits until the stored wake, at most an hour.\n\nSimplification: no required change. A later head can retire the per-pass diagnostic step list once the rate is measured.\n\nThis approval authorizes no push, landing or deployment. The planner decides the landing and runs the first hour's rate read against the ceiling in the note.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:9979755ca00f7fad87b252bd2ba2d57037ef574a","binding":"assigned","head":"aca376f750b29f13c858e254c135456caa27c5d9","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:79cbf34f28ef9e1d7af47de56eab17752d1c800f\"]","implementations":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:13bdd599f575b7e8e6389df32b948af886d0e2ec\"]","review_frontier":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:79cbf34f28ef9e1d7af47de56eab17752d1c800f","review_path":"reviewguard@1","